# AgentQ -- byte-exactness rules.
#
# The project's hard constraint is that skill/assets/unix/agentq-server and
# skill/assets/windows-git-bash/agentq are byte-identical (run-tests.sh check 01
# asserts `cmp` returns 0).  Line-ending translation would break that, and it
# would break it silently on a machine whose core.autocrlf is not `false` --
# which is the common case on Windows, where these assets are actually used.
#
# So: never translate.  Every file is stored and checked out exactly as it is.
# `* -text` is the only thing that protects 10 of the 25 deployment files
# (SKILL.md, agents/openai.yaml, the three extension-less unix/client scripts,
# the two plists, the .service and both pueue.yml): the per-extension rules
# below cover only .sh/.bash/.ps1/.cmd.  Measured 2026-10-26 on git 2.55.0:
# with this line removed, core.autocrlf=true checks the 60-byte wrapper out
# with `#!/bin/sh\r` and core.autocrlf=input stores a CRLF file as LF in the
# index.  `smoke/01` asserts the resolved value, not this text.
* -text

# Shell and PowerShell sources keep their own line endings verbatim.
*.sh   -text
*.bash -text
*.ps1  -text
*.cmd  -text

# The two canonical server copies.  `-merge` is what keeps a textual three-way
# merge from rewriting them: measured 2026-10-26 on git 2.55.0, two branches
# changing different lines of the same file produce `CONFLICT ... Cannot merge
# binary files` with `-merge` set, and a silent auto-merge without it -- while
# a one-sided change still merges cleanly.  (`-diff` only affects how `git
# diff` displays the file; it does NOT stop the merge, measured.)
skill/assets/unix/agentq-server          -text -diff -merge
skill/assets/windows-git-bash/agentq     -text -diff -merge
